This package, and the two that share its name
=============================================

This is matomo-vanilla, from debian.matomo.org. It ships Matomo exactly as
upstream releases it, bundled libraries included, and follows upstream's
releases.

Debian ships its own package, called matomo. It is built from the same
upstream source but replaces Matomo's bundled libraries with Debian ones and
follows the Debian release cycle rather than upstream's. Both install into
/usr/share/matomo and can never be installed at the same time.

Until Matomo 5.3.1 this package was also called matomo, and the collision was
silent: apt would pick whichever of the two carried the higher version number,
and could replace one with the other during an ordinary upgrade without saying
anything. Distinct names make that choice yours. The last package published
under the old name, matomo 1:5.3.1-2, only announces the rename; installing
this package removes it, and once Matomo answers correctly you can purge it.

Install
=======

1 - Create a MariaDB / MySQL database named "matomo"
	CREATE DATABASE `matomo` /*!40100 DEFAULT CHARACTER SET utf8 */;

2 - Create a user/password with access to the database previously created
	CREATE USER matomo@localhost IDENTIFIED BY '4/V3ryStron6!*passW0rd';
	GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON matomo.* to matomo@localhost;
	-- the FILE privileges is optional, but matomo can leverage that feature
	-- to improve performance
	GRANT FILE ON *.* TO matomo@localhost;

3a- If you don't have any virtualhost, say yes to the package's question about
serving Matomo at /matomo (dpkg-reconfigure matomo-vanilla asks it again), or
enable the alias yourself: "a2enconf matomo-alias" on Apache, "lighty-enable-mod
matomo" on lighttpd. Until you have completed the web installer, that address
lets anyone who reaches the server set Matomo up, so do it right after.

3b- If you operate with virtualhosts, do as follow
	a) if you use a dedicated virtual host for your matomo
	<VirtualHost *:80>
		[...]
		DocumentRoot /usr/share/matomo
		[...]
	</VirtualHost>

	b) if you want the matomo Alias in all your virtualhosts, uncomment the Alias line in /etc/apache2/conf.d/matomo.conf

	c) if you prefer to restrict matomo Alias to a specific virtualhost
	<VirtualHost *:80>
		[...]
		DocumentRoot /var/www/foo

		Alias /matomo /usr/share/matomo
		[...]
	</VirtualHost>

3c- Ensure conf-available/matomo.conf is enable

4 - Point your browser to your matomo URL

5 - Follow the steps and provide the MariaDB / MySQL credential details when
requested.

6 - Add the given javascript code to your pages, ideally at the bottom of the
html just before </body>

See http://matomo.org/docs/installation/

Automatic archiving
===================

Archiving is driven by a systemd timer, matomo.timer, and the package asks
whether to enable it rather than leaving you to remember. Which question it
asks depends on what it finds running:

  - archiving already running from a cron job: keep the cron job, or hand
    over to the timer. Keeping it puts the file back exactly as it was, and
    the timer stays inactive;

  - nothing running: enable the timer now, or not;

  - a timer already enabled, or a timer drop-in of your own under /etc:
    nothing is asked and nothing is touched.

Answering yes on a machine where Matomo has not been set up yet is safe: a
run with nothing configured does nothing and reports success, and archiving
begins by itself once you finish the web installer.

To change your mind:

	sudo dpkg-reconfigure matomo-vanilla
	sudo systemctl enable --now matomo.timer     # or disable --now
	systemctl list-timers matomo.timer

What the timer archives: every site of this installation, which is what
core:archive does on its own. It is all of them or none -- enabling the timer
or not is the whole choice the package offers.

Anything finer is a drop-in of your own, and it is upstream's options that do
the work, not anything this package provides:

	sudo systemctl edit matomo.service

	[Service]
	ExecStart=
	ExecStart=/usr/bin/php /usr/share/matomo/console core:archive \
	          --force-idsites=1,4

  --force-idsites=LIST   archive only these site ids (comma separated)
  --skip-idsites=LIST    archive everything except these
  --skip-all-segments    leave segments alone
  --url=URL              an explicit base URL, if the CLI needs one here

Clearing ExecStart before setting it is required: without the empty assignment
a drop-in adds a second command rather than replacing the first. The same
mechanism is how matomo-vanilla-logfile duplicates the output into a file, so
if you keep both drop-ins, yours wins and the log file stops being written --
copy the tee from
/usr/lib/systemd/system/matomo.service.d/10-logfile.conf into yours.


Up to 5.3.1-1 this was /etc/cron.d/matomo-archive, shipped commented out. The
file belongs to the matomo package, which is removed rather than purged when
this one replaces it, so it stays on disk until you purge matomo. If its
archiving line was active you were asked whether to keep it or to move to the
timer; moving comments the line out, so that the two do not both archive.

See https://matomo.org/faq/how-to/how-do-i-setup-auto-archiving-script-to-run-
using-systemd-on-a-linux-system-instead-of-cron/

Updating Matomo
===============

Matomo is updated by updating this package. Matomo's own one-click updater is
off by default: this package sets

	enable_auto_update = 0

in /etc/matomo/common.config.ini.php. Matomo merges three files in order --
upstream's config/global.ini.php from the code tree, then that one, then your
own /etc/matomo/config.ini.php -- so this is a default, not a constraint.
Anything you set in config.ini.php wins, and Matomo never writes to the file
this package ships.

The reason for the default: apt is in charge of the code, and the one-click
updater would replace the files dpkg is responsible for. dpkg's record would
stop describing what is on disk, and the next package upgrade would leave the
two halves mixed.

Turning it on
-------------

It is a legitimate choice, and the setting alone is not enough. Matomo also
has to be able to write the code tree, which it cannot: /usr/share/matomo
belongs to root. Both steps, in your own file:

	sudo sed -i 's/^enable_auto_update *=.*/enable_auto_update = 1/' \
		/etc/matomo/config.ini.php     # add the line if it is not there
	sudo chown -R www-data:www-data /usr/share/matomo

With the setting on and the ownership unchanged, Matomo stops before writing
anything and names the directories it could not write ("Some folders are not
writable. Please ensure the following folders are writable and try again:
..."). Nothing is left half-updated -- but nothing is updated either, so do
both or neither.

Read "Ownership of /usr/share/matomo" below before you do: handing the tree to
the web server is the part that carries a cost, and that section says what it
is and how to undo it.

If the updater has already been used on this installation, matomo-vanilla will
refuse to install and will print what to do about it. It refuses before
touching anything, so nothing is lost by trying.

Ownership of /usr/share/matomo
==============================

The tree is meant to belong to root, not to the web server: Matomo is PHP, so
a tree writable by www-data is a tree whose code the web server can rewrite.
Only the few paths that Matomo genuinely writes to are handed over, and the
postinst sets them. If you find the whole tree owned by www-data, from an
older manual step or from enabling the one-click updater, put it back:

	sudo chown -R root:root /usr/share/matomo
	sudo dpkg-reconfigure matomo-vanilla

The second command lets the postinst restore the handful of paths that do need
to be writable. Directories you have set with dpkg-statoverride are left
alone.

Installing without apt
======================

No upgrade makes the switch: the name matomo stops at 1:5.3.1-2, which only
announces it. Ask for the new name, once:

	sudo apt install matomo-vanilla

It replaces matomo 1:5.3.1-2 and keeps /etc/matomo, /var/lib/matomo and the
database where they are. An older matomo from this repository is refused with
the instruction to upgrade it first: up to 5.3.1-1 its prerm deletes
/var/lib/matomo on removal, and 1:5.3.1-2 is the version that checks whether
Matomo's own updater has replaced the tree.

With dpkg alone:

	sudo dpkg -r matomo
	sudo dpkg -i matomo-vanilla_<version>_all.deb
